{"birds": [{"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 3, "first": "9:03 AM", "last": "9:04 AM", "ts": 1793801017, "mins": [543, 544, 544], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/gwAD/wYAA/8GAAf/PAAH/7wAB//8AA//+AAf//4AH/+/AD//vwA//38Af/98AP//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4APg/wADgH8ABwA+AA4APgAeAH4AHAB8ADgA/AAwAeAAMAPAAAAHwAAADwAAAA8AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 23, "first": "9:03 AM", "last": "9:14 AM", "ts": 1793801022, "mins": [543, 544, 544, 545, 545, 546, 546, 546, 547, 548, 548, 549, 549, 550, 550, 551, 551, 551, 551, 552, 553, 553, 554], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "White-breasted Nuthatch", "sci": "Sitta carolinensis", "n": 2, "first": "9:04 AM", "last": "9:05 AM", "ts": 1793801049, "mins": [544, 545], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "Nuthatches climb down tree trunks head first. The name comes from “nut-hack”: they wedge a seed into bark and hack it open.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113050", "snd": "sounds/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113095", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC14860, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "nuthatch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/nuthatch.png", "w": 532, "h": 327, "len": 372.0, "beak": [0.79, 0.42, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 20, "b": "fgAAAH8AAAB/AAAAP4AAAD+AAAAfwAAAH/4gAB///gAf///AB///gAf//gAD//wAA//4AAP/8AAB//AAAf/AAAD+AAAA/gAAAP4AAAB/AAA="}}, {"name": "Tufted Titmouse", "sci": "Baeolophus bicolor", "n": 1, "first": "9:05 AM", "last": "9:05 AM", "ts": 1793801121, "mins": [545], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Titmice line their nests with hair, and they have been seen plucking it from live raccoons, dogs, and even people.", "vox": "sounds/voice/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793112971", "snd": "sounds/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793113083", "sndby": "Jim Berry, xeno-canto XC649331, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "titmouse", "cm": 16.5, "img": "cutouts/titmouse.png", "w": 574, "h": 800, "len": 653.0, "beak": [0.2, 0.25, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 45, "b": "cAAAAHAAAAAwAAAAIAAAAGgDAAB8B4AAPB8AAD5/AAA+/4AAeP+AAHv/wAB7/8AA+P/gAPhn4ACbH/AADz/wAA9/+AAPf/wABb/8AB+//gAO//4ABt//AAfP/wAHx/+AB+P/gAPh/8AHiP/AB47/wAeH/+AHofvgBvOB8AD3APgAfAB4APgAPAD4AB4A+AAfAPwADwA3AA8APwAHAD4AAAAfAAAAGwAAABgAAAAMAAAADAAA"}}, {"name": "Northern Flicker", "sci": "Colaptes auratus", "n": 5, "first": "9:06 AM", "last": "9:11 AM", "ts": 1793801195, "mins": [546, 546, 548, 550, 551], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "A woodpecker that often feeds on the ground, mainly on ants. Eastern flickers flash bright yellow under the wings and tail when they fly.", "vox": "sounds/voice/colaptes-auratus.mp3?v=1793112986", "snd": "sounds/colaptes-auratus.mp3?v=1793113157", "sndby": "Thomas Magarian, xeno-canto XC354617, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "flicker", "cm": 31.0, "img": "cutouts/flicker.png", "w": 800, "h": 579, "len": 732.0, "beak": [0.66, 0.07, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "AA/AAAAP/AAAH/gAAD/AAAB/gAEA/4AHAf+ADgH/wDwD/4DwA/+B4Af/h4AH/x4AD/54AA//4AAf/4AAH98AAD79AAA+8AAAP/AAAH8gAAD+AAAA+AAAAPAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 1, "first": "9:09 AM", "last": "9:09 AM", "ts": 1793801386, "mins": [549], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 1, "first": "9:11 AM", "last": "9:11 AM", "ts": 1793801510, "mins": [551], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPgAAAD+AAAA/4AAAH+AAAD/8AAB//AAA//gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAB//4AAf/+AAf//gAH//zAD//5wA//94Af+OcAP/8eAD9/fgA/l/gAPgHwAHgD4AHwA4AB8AeAA+AGAAfADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}], "also": [], "date": "Wednesday, November 4", "where": "", "updated": "9:14 AM", "sun": null, "code": "3bb51669b7", "listen": {"